Lab 6: Self-Healing Systems with Kubernetes and AI
Objective
Deploy a Kubernetes cluster, train an AI model to detect system anomalies, and implement an automated self-healing system that resizes or repairs pods based on AI insights.

Prerequisites
Ubuntu Machine with Docker installed
Install Docker:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install docker.io -y
Install Minikube (for local Kubernetes):
curl -Lo minikube https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64
chmod +x minikube
sudo mv minikube /usr/local/bin/
Start Minikube:
minikube start --driver=docker
Install Kubectl:
sudo apt install kubectl -y
Steps
Step 1: Deploy Kubernetes Cluster
Check cluster status:
kubectl cluster-info
Deploy a sample app (e.g., Nginx or a custom app):
kubectl create deployment webapp --image=nginx
kubectl expose deployment webapp --type=NodePort --port=80
Scale the deployment:
kubectl scale deployment webapp --replicas=3
Check pods and services:
kubectl get pods
kubectl get svc
Step 2: Train an AI Model for Anomaly Detection
Install ML libraries:
pip install pandas scikit-learn tensorflow
Simulate data for AI model (e.g., CPU/Memory usage metrics):
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np
from sklearn.ensemble import IsolationForest

# Simulated system metrics (CPU, Memory usage)
np.random.seed(42)
data = {
    "cpu_usage": np.random.normal(50, 5, 1000),  # Normal CPU usage (50%)
    "memory_usage": np.random.normal(60, 10, 1000)  # Normal memory usage (60%)
}
# Simulate anomalies (outliers)
data["cpu_usage"][200:250] = np.random.normal(90, 5, 50)  # High CPU usage anomaly
data["memory_usage"][400:450] = np.random.normal(20, 5, 50)  # Low memory usage anomaly

df = pd.DataFrame(data)

# Train an anomaly detection model (Isolation Forest)
model = IsolationForest(contamination=0.05)
model.fit(df)

# Predict anomalies
df["anomaly"] = model.predict(df)
df["anomaly"] = df["anomaly"].map({1: 0, -1: 1})  # 1 for anomaly, 0 for normal
print(df.head())
Save the trained model:
import joblib
joblib.dump(model, 'anomaly_detection_model.pkl')
Step 3: Integrate AI with Kubernetes for Self-Healing
Create a Python script to predict anomalies from Kubernetes metrics:
import kubernetes
import joblib
import numpy as np
from kubernetes import client, config
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

# Load pre-trained anomaly detection model
model = joblib.load('anomaly_detection_model.pkl')

# Initialize Kubernetes client
config.load_kube_config()  # If running locally
v1 = client.CoreV1Api()

# Get pod metrics (simulate using a Python script or use Prometheus)
# Example: CPU and memory usage for a pod (you can pull real metrics from your Kubernetes setup)
cpu_usage = np.random.normal(50, 5)  # Replace with actual CPU usage from metrics server
memory_usage = np.random.normal(60, 10)  # Replace with actual memory usage

# Predict anomaly using the AI model
data = np.array([[cpu_usage, memory_usage]])
scaler = StandardScaler()
data_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(data)
anomaly = model.predict(data_scaled)

if anomaly == -1:
    print("Anomaly detected! Triggering self-healing process.")
    # Self-healing: Automatically rescale or restart pods based on AI insight
    # Example: Restart pod if anomaly detected
    pod_name = "webapp-1234"  # Replace with actual pod name
    v1.delete_namespaced_pod(name=pod_name, namespace="default")
    print(f"Pod {pod_name} deleted and being rescheduled.")
Automate healing:

If an anomaly is detected, trigger rescaling or restarting pods:
kubectl scale deployment webapp --replicas=5  # Rescale to 5 replicas
